    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d92">

                <pb facs="0098" n="92" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg92"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/1900: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-10-03T23:00:00+08:00">October 3, 1939—11
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 3—10:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>885. Reference to Department’s telegram No. 379, September 30, 1 p.m., and 382,
                    October 2, 8 p.m. I am seeing Japanese Consul General tomorrow and leaving with
                    him a memorandum.<anchor n="91" xml:id="d92-f1"/> Embassy at Tokyo has been
                    requested to take action. American Commandant tells me British military not yet
                    prepared to take action regarding proposals for revision of defense plan.
                    Growing indications suggest that their position when finally determined upon may
                    be weak.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Peiping and Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d92-f1" n="91" xml:id="d92-f1-content">
                <p>British representations were made October 7.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
